<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/19th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Campaign</p><h1 class="enc-title">Invasion of the Waikato</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1863">1863</a>–<a href="/years/1864">1864</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Invasion of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/waikato">Waikato</a> was the largest campaign of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-zealand">New Zealand</a> Wars, fought in 1863–1864 by British and colonial forces against the Māori King Movement in the Waikato region of the North Island. After battles at Rangiriri and Ōrākau the King Movement withdrew south, and the government confiscated large areas of Māori land.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/invasion-of-the-waikato.jpg" alt="Invasion of the Waikato" width="320" height="212" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: James Cowan · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ngati_maniapoto_survivors_of_the_war.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The First Taranaki War ended in March 1861 in an uneasy truce. Governor Thomas Gore Browne then sent the Kīngitanga, centred on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/waikato">Waikato</a>, an ultimatum demanding submission to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/queen-victoria">Queen Victoria</a> and, when it was rejected, drew up plans to invade and depose King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tawhiao">Tāwhiao</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/colonial-office">Colonial Office</a> and the General Assembly opposed them, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-grey">Sir George Grey</a> suspended them in December 1861. Grey revived the plans in June 1863 amid fears of a Kingite raid on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/auckland">Auckland</a>, which historians such as James Belich regard as fear-mongering, and on 9 July demanded that all Māori between Auckland and the Waikato swear allegiance or be expelled south of the Waikato River.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>Troops under Cameron, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crimean-war">Crimean War</a> veteran who had replaced Major-General Thomas Pratt as British commander-in-chief, crossed into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/waikato">Waikato</a> three days later and on 17 July attacked an unfinished entrenchment at Koheroa, near Mercer, where Te Huirama, a relative of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tawhiao">Tāwhiao</a>, was among about 15 Māori killed. The same day a war party ambushed a convoy at Martin's Farm on the Great South Road. On 20 November Cameron attacked the line at Rangiriri, engineered by the chief Te Wharepu, in the costliest engagement of the wars, and took 180 Māori prisoners.</p><p>The Kingites then built a still stronger line centred on Paterangi, held by 1200 to 2000 men from a dozen iwi. Guided by Himi Manuao and John Gage, Cameron outflanked it towards Te Awamutu and Rangiaowhia. At Ōrākau, built at the urging of Tūhoe chiefs against Rewi Maniapoto's judgement, the garrison held for three days from 31 March 1864, short of water and firing peach stones and wood when their lead ran out, until the British sap came within grenade range and an Armstrong gun breached the wall. Troop numbers peaked at about 14,000 in March 1864, supplied by the Commissariat Transport Corps.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>Cameron then faced Te Tiki o te Ihingarangi, part of the Kingite line called the aukati, or boundary; its defenders left it after three days, but he judged further effort fruitless and turned to Tauranga. The land behind the aukati became known as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/king-country">King Country</a>. Under the New Zealand Settlements Act of December 1863 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/waikato">Waikato</a> land was confiscated, and evicted owners saw their houses ransacked and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a> seized. In 1995 the government conceded that the invasion and confiscation had been wrongful and apologised, Waikato–Tainui accepted a settlement worth about $171 million, and Queen <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elizabeth-ii">Elizabeth II</a> signed the Waikato Raupatu Claims Settlement Act 1995 herself.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Invasion of the Waikato” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
